---
layout: layouts/page
title: AI/AN Medicaid exemption scorecard
permalink: /aian-medicaid-scorecard/
description: How closely each state's published Medicaid policy follows CMS rules for American Indians and Alaska Natives under the new work and renewal requirements.
---
{% set sc = aianScorecard %}
{% from "aian-dots.njk" import dots %}
{% if not sc.rubric.approved %}
<div class="usa-alert usa-alert--warning usa-alert--slim"><div class="usa-alert__body"><p class="usa-alert__text"><strong>Draft.</strong> Grades are provisional while the rubric is under review and evidence is re-verified.</p></div></div>
{% endif %}

<h1>American Indian and Alaska Native Medicaid exemption scorecard</h1>
<p class="aian-disclaimer"><strong>Grades are our sourced assessment as of {{ sc.lastChecked }}.</strong> We will be wrong sometimes, and we correct quickly when shown published policy. <a href="{{ sc.rubric.submission.form_url }}">Send us a published policy link</a>, or see <a href="{{ '/aian-medicaid-scorecard/process/' | url }}">how corrections work</a>. Each state's page lists every change we have made.</p>
<p class="aian-disclaimer" id="round-down"><strong>If there's any question, we round down.</strong> {{ sc.rubric.round_down | replace("If there's any question, we round down. ", "") }}</p>
{% set sm = sc.summary %}
{% set rq = sc.rubric.requirements | selectattr("interpretation") | first %}
<div class="usa-alert usa-alert--info usa-alert--slim aian-cms-conflict"><div class="usa-alert__body"><p class="usa-alert__text"><strong>A conflict in CMS's own rules.</strong> {{ rq.interpretation }} Sources: {% for x in rq.interpretation_sources %}<a href="{{ x.url }}">{{ x.label }}</a>{% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}.</p></div></div>
<p class="usa-intro aian-headline">Only <strong>{{ sm.allMet }} of {{ sm.subject }}</strong> states meet all {{ sm.total }} requirements in their published policy. The median state meets <strong>{{ sm.median }}</strong>, and <strong>{{ sm.anyContradiction }}</strong> publish policy that contradicts at least one.</p>

<p>Federal law exempts American Indians and Alaska Natives from Medicaid's new work requirement and keeps their renewals at 12 months. This scorecard grades how closely each state's <strong>published</strong> policy follows the CMS rules that carry out those protections. It measures what states have written down, not what happens at every eligibility office.</p>

<p>Grades update as states publish new policy. A state, tribe, or Indian health program can <a href="{{ '/aian-medicaid-scorecard/process/' | url }}">send a published policy link</a> from any state's page. Last checked {{ sc.lastChecked }}.</p>

<h2>By the numbers</h2>
<ul class="usa-list">
  <li><strong>{{ sm.allMet }} of {{ sm.subject }}</strong> states subject to the new rules meet all {{ sm.total }} requirements in their published policy.</li>
  <li>The median state meets <strong>{{ sm.median }} of {{ sm.total }}</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>{{ sm.anyContradiction }}</strong> states publish policy that contradicts at least one requirement.</li>
  <li><strong>{{ sm.anyDisputed }}</strong> states publish that they check status again at renewal. The regulation allows that, but CMS's preamble says states may not reverify, so we mark it <strong>disputed</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>{{ sm.tribalChannel }}</strong> states publish a way for tribes or Indian health programs to confirm a person's status.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="how-grades-change">How a state raises its grade</h2>
<ol class="usa-list">
  <li><strong>Publish a qualifying policy.</strong> Each state's page lists every requirement it does not yet meet, the policy that would meet it, model language from a state that already does, and the grade the state would reach.</li>
  <li><strong>Send us the published policy link.</strong> Use the Send a published policy link button on the state's page. A state agency, tribe, Indian health program, or anyone else can submit, and evidence from tribes and Indian health programs counts the same as the state's.</li>
  <li><strong>We review within {{ sc.rubric.submission.review_business_days }} business days.</strong> The state's page shows Evidence under review until we finish.</li>
  <li><strong>The grade updates.</strong> An upgrade publishes right away. A downgrade waits {{ sc.rubric.submission.downgrade_notice_days }} days after we notify the state. Every change is listed in the state's history with its date and source.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Grades at a glance</h2>
<ul class="usa-list">
{% for c in sc.counts %}{% if c.n > 0 %}<li><strong>{{ c.grade }}</strong>: {{ c.n }} {% if c.n == 1 %}state{% else %}states{% endif %}</li>{% endif %}{% endfor %}
</ul>

<h2>All states, ranked</h2>
<p>Ranked by requirements met, then fewest contradicted, then fewest unpublished. See <a href="#rubric">How grades work</a> and <a href="#terms">Terms used</a>.</p>
{% set im = sc.rubric.impact %}
<ul class="usa-list aian-legend">
  <li><strong>Score</strong>: how many of the {{ sc.summary.total }} requirements the state's published policy meets.</li>
  <li><strong>Impact</strong>: roughly how many people could be exempt. Each {{ dots(1, 0) }} is about {{ im.dot_size | aianNum }} people, and {{ dots(0, 0.5) }} means fewer than {{ im.dot_size | aianNum }}. <a href="#impact-method">How we estimate this</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>To fix</strong>: every requirement the state does not meet, contradictions first. Each links to the fix on the state's page.</li>
</ul>
<table class="usa-table usa-table--borderless usa-table--stacked aian-roster">
  <caption class="usa-sr-only">States ranked by requirements met, with grade, estimated impact, and the requirements each state still needs to fix</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">Score <span class="aian-th-note">met of {{ sc.summary.total }}</span></th><th scope="col">Grade</th><th scope="col">Impact <span class="aian-th-note">people, modeled estimate (approx.)</span></th><th scope="col">To fix</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
  {% for s in sc.ranked %}
    {% set url = ('/aian-medicaid-scorecard/' + s.slug + '/') | url %}
    <tr>
      <th scope="row" data-label="State"><a href="{{ url }}">{{ s.name }}</a>{% if s.under_review %} <span class="usa-tag">Evidence under review</span>{% endif %}</th>
      <td class="aian-col-score" data-label="Score (met of {{ sc.summary.total }})">{% if s.status != 'not_subject' %}<strong>{{ s.met }}/{{ sc.summary.total }}</strong><ul class="aian-counts"><li>{{ s.contradicts }} contradicted</li>{% if s.disputed %}<li>{{ s.disputed }} disputed</li>{% endif %}<li>{{ s.unpublished }} unpublished</li></ul>{% endif %}</td>
      <td class="aian-col-grade" data-label="Grade">{{ s.grade }}{% if s.status == 'not_subject' and not s.confirmed %} (to confirm){% endif %}</td>
      <td class="aian-col-impact" data-label="Impact (modeled, approx.)">{% if s.status != 'not_subject' %}{{ dots(s.impact.full, s.impact.part) }}<br><span class="aian-impact-n">{{ s.impact.estimate | aianNum }}</span>{% if s.impact.published %}<br><span class="text-base aian-impact-pub">State reports {{ s.impact.published.count | aianNum }} <a href="{{ s.impact.published.url }}">already verified as exempt</a></span>{% endif %}{% endif %}</td>
      <td class="aian-col-fix" data-label="To fix">{% if s.status == 'not_subject' %}Not subject{% elif not s.improvements.length %}Nothing{% else %}{% set con = s.fixContradicts %}{% set dis = s.fixDisputed %}{% set unp = s.fixUnpublished %}{% if con.length %}<span class="aian-fix-kind">Contradicts:</span> {% for i in con %}<a href="{{ url }}#fix-{{ i.id }}">{{ i.short }}</a>{% if not loop.last %} · {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endif %}{% if con.length and (dis.length or unp.length) %}<br>{% endif %}{% if dis.length %}<span class="aian-fix-kind">Disputed:</span> {% for i in dis %}<a href="{{ url }}#fix-{{ i.id }}">{{ i.short }}</a>{% if not loop.last %} · {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endif %}{% if dis.length and unp.length %}<br>{% endif %}{% if unp.length %}<span class="aian-fix-kind">Not published:</span> {% for i in unp %}<a href="{{ url }}#fix-{{ i.id }}">{{ i.short }}</a>{% if not loop.last %} · {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endif %}{% endif %}</td>
    </tr>
  {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="rubric">How grades work</h2>
<ul class="usa-list">
  <li>Each state is checked against {{ sc.rubric.requirements.length }} requirements from federal law.</li>
  <li>For each requirement, the state's published policy either <strong>meets</strong> it, <strong>contradicts</strong> it, or says nothing (<strong>not published</strong>).</li>
  <li>One more mark applies only to re-checks: <strong>disputed</strong>, for a published check at renewal. The regulation allows it but CMS's preamble says states may not reverify. Disputed counts neither as met nor as a contradiction. See the note on CMS's conflicting rules at the top of this page.</li>
  <li>Saying nothing never counts as meeting a requirement.</li>
  <li><strong>If there's any question, we round down.</strong> A requirement is met only when the state's published words clearly commit to it. It is contradicted only when the state's words clearly apply to American Indians and Alaska Natives and conflict with the federal rule. Hedged (such as &ldquo;may&rdquo; or &ldquo;some adults&rdquo;), implied, or general wording counts as not published. We don't adjudicate what a state meant; the state can clarify its own published policy.</li>
  <li>The grade follows from those counts:</li>
</ul>
<table class="usa-table usa-table--borderless">
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Grade</th><th scope="col">Rule</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{% for g in sc.rubric.grades %}<tr><td>{{ g.grade }}</td><td>{{ g.rule }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody>
</table>
<p>Not graded: {% for b in sc.rubric.best_practices %}we also report whether a state offers {{ b.label | lower }}. It is a tribal recommendation, not a CMS requirement, so it does not affect the grade.{% endfor %}</p>

<h2 id="reading-wording">How we read "eligible for Indian Health Service care"</h2>
<p>Many states exempt people "eligible for Indian Health Service care" without listing each group the federal rule covers. We read that wording this way:</p>
<ul class="usa-list">
  <li>It <strong>meets</strong> IHS-eligible, Alaska Natives, and California Indians. The federal definition of Indian (<a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/42/447.51">42 CFR 447.51</a>) counts anyone the Secretary of Health and Human Services considers an Indian for Indian health care, "including as a California Indian, Eskimo, Aleut, or other Alaska Native." California Indians are eligible for Indian Health Service care by statute (<a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/25/1679">25 U.S.C. 1679(a)</a>).</li>
  <li>It <strong>meets</strong> Tribal members. Indian Health Service rules list tribal membership and enrollment as evidence of eligibility (<a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/42/136.12">42 CFR 136.12</a>).</li>
  <li>It does <strong>not</strong> meet Urban Indians and descendants. Urban Indian programs serve people, such as descendants and members of state-recognized tribes, who may not be eligible for Indian Health Service care. A state meets that requirement only by naming them or by adopting the full federal definition.</li>
</ul>
<p>A state can remove any doubt by publishing the full federal definition. Each state's page links the fix.</p>

<h2 id="impact-method">How we estimate impact</h2>
<ul class="usa-list">
  <li>We start from each state's 2020 Census count of people who identify as American Indian or Alaska Native alone.</li>
  <li>We assume {{ (im.subject_share * 100) | round }}% of them are adults in the Medicaid expansion group, the people the new rules cover. No published source gives that share, so this is our estimate.</li>
  <li>Each {{ dots(1, 0) }} is {{ im.dot_size | aianNum }} people, rounded down. A state under {{ im.dot_size | aianNum }} shows {{ dots(0, 0.5) }}.</li>
  <li>Where a state has reported its own count, we show it beside the estimate. The two measure different things: our estimate is everyone who may qualify, while a state count is people the state has already verified. The gap is not a count of people missed.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="terms">Terms used</h2>
<ul class="usa-list">
  <li><strong>Expansion group</strong>: adults aged 19 to 64 who qualify for Medicaid because their state expanded it under the Affordable Care Act. The new work requirement and six-month renewals apply only to this group.</li>
  <li><strong>Work requirement</strong>: the rule that some adults show work or other activities to keep Medicaid. Federal rules call it the community engagement requirement. Alaska calls it WCER, for work and community engagement requirements.</li>
  <li><strong>Renewal</strong> (also called redetermination): the state's regular check that someone still qualifies for Medicaid.</li>
  <li><strong>Published policy</strong>: anything the state has made public, such as a web page, manual, notice, form, plan amendment, or waiver.</li>
  <li><strong>Not subject</strong>: a state that has not expanded Medicaid, so these rules do not apply there.</li>
</ul>

